I. Introduction
T
HERE are two important aspects in the analysis of rotorcraft dynamics. They are 1) aeroelastic loads and response studies, and 2) flight dynamics studies related to stability, control response, and handling qualities. A vast amount of literature is available on aeroelastic loads and response studies; but, only a few studies are available in the open literature on helicopter flight dynamics and handling qualities evaluation. It is interesting to note that several comprehensive analyses [1–3] extensively addressed the important aspects of aeroelastic loads and response from the point of view of
helicopter vibration. Sophisticated structural modeling of elastic blade and aerodynamic modeling, including compressibility, reverse flow, and stall effects, is included in the comprehensive studies. The mathematical models for flight dynamics usually have relatively simple structural and aerodynamic models with a rigid-blade assumption [4–7]; but, the flight dynamics formulation is more general, so that various maneuvering flight conditions can be analyzed. Although many of the flight dynamics studies focused on hover and steady 1g level forward-flight conditions, only a few studies addressed high-g maneuvering flight conditions [8–15]. Unlike fixed-wing aircraft, high-g maneuvers for helicopters are quite complicated due to the changes in magnitude and the direction of rotor thrust. The prediction of maneuverability limits of a helicopter deals with analyzing or determining 1) the ability of the helicopter rotor to change the direction of thrust within the available power and energy constraints; and 2) the ability of the rotor to produce the thrust required for maneuver. Due to retreating blade stall during steep high-g maneuvers, flight measurements usually exceed the analytical predictions by a considerable margin. Inclusion of the stall effect in the rotor aerodynamics is needed for better maneuver capability predictions [16]. Chen [8] and Chen and Jeske [9] examined the kinematic properties of rotary-wing and fixed-wing aircraft in steady coordinated turns in detail. Various load factor definitions were interpreted, and it was concluded that the total load factor was the most logical definition for helicopters. The total load factor is the ratio of the total aerodynamic and propulsive force to the weight of the aircraft. In general, a coordinated turn is defined as the turn maneuver in which the total aerodynamic and propulsive forces acting on the aircraft lie in the plane of the x-z body axis (total external forces in the lateral direction are zero). For a fixed-wing aircraft, the sideslip angle of the vehicle is also zero in a steady coordinated turn. But, in the case of rotorcraft (conventional single main rotor helicopter), sideslip is inherently exhibited in a steady coordinated turn maneuver [10]. The roll rate and the pitch attitude are strongly influenced by the sideslip, which further influences the performance and handling qualities of helicopter. Because a precise coordinated turn is rarely achieved in the conventional rotorcraft, the knowledge of an uncoordinated steady turn is also of great interest. The turn is said to be uncoordinated if the lateral load factor is not equal to zero in the body-fixed frame. Chen [11] developed computational algorithms to calculate the trim states and small perturbation equation of motion for fixed-wing and rotarywing vehicles in coordinated and uncoordinated, steady, steep turn maneuvers. These algorithms are applied to investigate the flight dynamics of a tiltrotor and two single-rotor helicopters in various levels of uncoordinated and high-g turn maneuvers [12,13]. In both coordinated and uncoordinated turns, strong coupling exists in lateral and longitudinal motions for both symmetric and asymmetric configurations. Stability of the conventional helicopter is strongly influenced by the direction of turn. Celi [14] studied the aeroelastic stability of a soft-in-plane hingeless rotor helicopter under a coordinated turn maneuver. It was concluded that the steady level turns stabilized the lag modes due to the increase in aerodynamic damping. But, the flap mode stability decreased in steep maneuvers and, for better flight dynamic predictions in high-g maneuvers, the aerodynamic model may be improved by adding blade stall effects. Guglieri and Celi [15] studied the flight dynamics and handling qualities of a hingeless rotor helicopter during a steady turn and analyzed the frequency responses in pitch, roll, and heave for various advance ratios and turn rates. It was observed that the stable zeros in straight flight condition were converted into a nonminimum phase in high-g maneuvers, and it resulted in degradation of handling qualities during steep high-g maneuvers.
