There are some interesting observations made in the formulation and solution of a mathematical model that governs the helicopter motion under general maneuvering conditions. In general, a helicopter under any general flight condition has 13 trim parameters, and they are 1) four control angles of θ0, θ1c, θ1s and θtr; 2) two Euler
angles of Θ and Φ; 3) three angular rates of p, q, and r; and 4) the velocity of the helicopter Vf, turn rate Ωa, flight-path angle γf, and
flight track angle χe. Whereas the number of equations is only nine, there are three force equations, three moment equations, and three kinematic relations. To handle this problem in a deterministic manner, four trim parameters (namely, vehicle velocity, flight-path angle, turn rate, and flight track angle) need to be prescribed. The last parameter, the flight track angle, may be specified explicitly or can be evaluated by imposing specific conditions on either the sideslip angle or lateral load factor. There is very little literature available (Padfield [17], Venkatesan [18], and Rohin Kumar [19]) in which the flight track angle is used as the unknown parameter in the trim computation. Apart from the previously discussed parameters, there is also another parameter known as the incidence angle α that was used as an unknown trim parameter in earlier studies [8–14] in place of the flight track angle. A simple algebraic expression that related the incidence angle to the flight track angle was derived in this study. It is interesting to note that the same flight condition can be mathematically solved by imposing a known value (usually zero) to any one of the three parameters, namely, 1) sideslip angle βs, 2) flight track angle χe, and
3) lateral load factor ny. Once the trim solution is obtained for the given condition of one parameter, the other two parameters can be evaluated for that flight condition from trim quantities. These results show that there are certain very specific flight conditions under which any two among the three conditions can be satisfied simultaneously. It has been brought out in this study that, by imposing the conditions of a 90 deg sideslip angle and zero lateral load factor simultaneously, a pirouettelike flight condition can be achieved. The objectives of this study are therefore 1) to develop a flight dynamic model and construct an efficient algorithm for trim computation for a conventional helicopter in general maneuvering flight condition, and 2) to study the influence of three mathematical conditions (zero sideslip angle, zero track angle, and zero lateral load factor) independently on the trim values for the same steady-state maneuvering flight condition.
