D. Solution Procedure for Trim
In a steady flight condition, the rates of change of the body translational velocities (u_, v_, w_ ), angular velocities (p_, q_, r_), and
Euler angles ( _Θ, Φ_ ) are set equal to zero in Eqs. (1–3). The trim computation of a rotorcraft under a steady turn maneuver involves 13 unknown parameters. Because the number of equations is only nine [Eqs. (1–3)], four parameters have to be prescribed. They are 1) vehicle velocity; 2) flight-path angle; 3) turn rate, and 4) flight track angle. The flight track angle can be prescribed in three ways, which are discussed later in this section. With prescribed flight parameters, the unknown trim parameters are reduced to nine. The nine equations are solved for the trim parameters, which are three main rotor control angles (θ0, θ1c, θ1s), the tail rotor collective θtr, the pitch attitude Θ, the roll attitude Φ, and the three angular rates (p, q, and r).
The flowchart for the helicopter trim computation under general maneuvering flight condition is shown in Fig. 2. With the prescribed flight conditions and initial guess of trim parameters, the computation starts with the evaluation of the flight track angle. Body translational velocities and angular velocities are obtained with the initial Euler angle guess and the computed flight track angle using Eqs. (3) and (4). This information is passed to the inner loop in which the main rotor loads are calculated along with the blade flap response and rotor inflow. The loads, inflow, and flap response are calculated at every 5 deg azimuth location of blade movement. The inner loop runs until the convergence of the blade flap response, and convergence is checked over a complete revolution at 72 azimuthal locations. It may be noted that the responses of all blades in the rotor system are solved simultaneously. Converged loads are then averaged over one revolution and transferred to the center of gravity of the helicopter. The tail rotor, fuselage, and empennage loads are calculated and transferred to the helicopter c.g. and added to the main rotor loads to obtain total c.g. loads (X, Y, Z, L, M, and N). These loads are passed to the trim solver, in which the flight dynamics equations [Eqs. (1) and (2)] are solved by a Newton–Raphson algorithm (outer loop).
