III. Results and Discussion
The results presented in this section are pertaining to a helicopter configuration of the 4–5 ton class, given in Tables 1 and 2. The direction of the main rotor rotation is counterclockwise when viewed from the top. The results are generated for various values of the steady turn rate. The positive turn rate corresponds to a right turn, whereas a negative turn rate corresponds to a left turn (from pilot’s view).
A. Trim Parameters
Trim parameters are obtained for the helicopter under the steady turn maneuvering flight condition using all the three mathematical conditions. The trim results are presented under three parts. In the first part, results pertaining to zero sideslip angle and zero track angle cases are presented. Because the lateral load factor can be nonzero, these two cases can be called an uncoordinated turn. The second part discusses the results pertaining to the zero lateral load factor case, which is denoted as a coordinated turn. In the third part, the results pertaining to specific flight conditions under which any two of the three conditions (sideslip angle, track angle, and lateral load factor) can be simultaneously satisfied.
1. Uncoordinated Turn
The trim parameters using zero sideslip and zero track angle cases are obtained for different turn rates at an advance ratio of 0.14, and they are shown in Figs. 3 and 4. In these figures, the circle symbols represent the zero sideslip case and the square symbols correspond to the zero track angle case. From Fig. 3, it can be seen that the trim parameters for both the cases almost coincide, except pitch attitude. A very small difference is observed in the pitch attitude between the zero sideslip and zero track angle cases (Fig. 3e). The main rotor collective, lateral cyclic, tail rotor collective, and pitch attitude show a positive increase with the turn rate for both right and left turns. The roll attitude also increases with the turn rate, but the sign of the roll attitude changes with the direction of the turn (positive roll attitude for right turn and negative roll attitude for left turn). It is interesting to note that, even though the roll attitude sign changes between the left and right turns, the lateral cyclic (Fig. 3b) is always positive. That implies that the lateral stick is always on one side (retreating side) with respect to the level flight condition. Figure 4 illustrates the variation of the flight track angle, sideslip angle, lateral load factor, and incidence angle for zero sideslip and zero flight track angle cases for the advance ratio of 0.14. From Fig. 4a, it may be noted that, for the case of zero sideslip, a small value of track angle is observed. In the right turn, the flight track angle increases and then decreases as the turn rate increases. The maximum flight track angle is 0.14 deg at the turn rate of 10 deg ∕s. In the left turn, the flight track angle first decreases and then increases as the turn rate increases, and it reaches a minimum of −0.186 deg at the turn rate of −10 deg ∕s. For the case of a zero track angle (Fig. 4b), a small nonzero sideslip angle is observed. In the right turn, the sideslip angle first decreases and then increases as the turn rate increases. The minimum sideslip angle is −0.16 deg at the turn rate of 10 deg ∕s. In the left turn, the sideslip angle increases and then decreases as the turn rate increases, and it reaches a maximum of 0.22 deg at the turn rate of −15 deg ∕s. From Fig. 4c, it is found that the lateral load factor coincides for both the cases, and it varies from 0.0455 to −0.0148. It may be noted that the helicopter is trimmed with a negative lateral load factor at high positive turn rates. It is observed that the incidence angle (Fig. 4d) also coincides for both zero sideslip and zero track angle cases.
