3. Special Cases
From Fig. 4c, it can be seen that lateral load factor is zero for a positive turn rate of 7.66 deg ∕s for both zero sideslip and zero track angle cases. It can also be seen from Figs. 4a and 4b that both the sideslip angle and track angle are zero at −25 and 20 deg ∕s turn rates. These observations indicate that there are specific flight conditions in which any two of the three conditions can be satisfied simultaneously. From Fig. 6b, it is interesting to note that the sideslip angle is close to 90 deg at a −15 deg ∕s turn rate. The trim parameters corresponding to these special flight conditions are given in Table 5. Case 1 satisfies both zero sideslip and zero lateral load factor conditions, and case 2 satisfies zero track angle and zero lateral load factor conditions. It is noted that the results corresponding to case 1 and case 2 are very close to each other in all the trim parameters. Case 3 corresponds to both zero sideslip and zero track angle conditions. Case 4 represents the flight condition corresponding to a 90 deg sideslip angle and zero lateral load factor. It is noted that the forward velocity and vertical velocity are close to zero, and the total flight velocity is represented by sideward velocity alone. Because the flight-path angle is already set to zero, the altitude remains constant. This particular flight condition resembles a pirouette maneuver. At an advance ratio of 0.14 and a turn rate of −15 deg ∕s, the helicopter is into the pirouette maneuver of 116.05 m radius and it completes one circle in 23.98 s. It is noted that this flight condition is applicable only for the negative turn rate. In the opinion of the authors, for the first time in the open literature, a mathematical condition for a pirouette maneuver of a helicopter has been identified and shown in this Note.
